{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjWameQ7c4jy5ATry1Zj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DEQE9krfMi9wtT3tbDCFRt","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with a treble clef and no key signature. First stretch, up to the first barline: two slurred eighths, G4 and F4, then four staccato eighths, F4 F4 G4 A4. That is bar 30, (G4 8th, F4 8th), F4 F4 G4 A4. Second stretch: D4 eighth, F4 quarter, then three beamed eighths D4 F4 D4. That is bar 31, D4 8th, F4 quarter, D4 8th, F4 8th, D4 8th. The copyist's slur over the whole bar isn't clearly visible, but the pitches and rhythm match. Third stretch: C4 quarter on a ledger line, an eighth rest, then repeat dots and a double barline. That is the first half of bar 32 (C4 quarter, rest 8th), up to the repeat sign that falls inside that bar. The rest of bar 32 (the dolce Eb5 D5 C5) and the key change to three flats will be on the next line. Nothing contradicts the edition.","right":true,"insights":"This line was easy to match. Bar 30's opening G4-F4 slur followed by repeated staccato F4s is distinctive, and bar 31's D4-F4 pattern is too. The repeat sign right after the C4 and eighth rest pins down the end of the line partway through bar 32. The only call needing care was counting that partial bar 32 as part of this line. Having the edition mark where in bar 32 the repeat falls (after the rest) made that easy, and it would have been easier still if the edition also marked the key change explicitly at that point."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5186,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5186},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"conte